    "q-34392": {
      "dom_id": "step-q34392",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}].$ Find $\\text{Null}(A).$",
      "worked_solution": "We need to solve the equation $Ax=0.$ First, we find the row echelon form of $A$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe second equation tells us that ${x}_{2}=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+2(0)=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=0.\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that $x=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ is the only solution to this system.\n\nTherefore, $\\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
